 }
 
+// DirectRaceEnabled gates whether the DIRECT outbound uses the address race at
+// all. It is a downstream switch, not an upstream one: the race, its candidate
+// cache and its progressive resolver exist only on this fork's dev branch, and
+// the upstream Alpha branch simply dials DIRECT destinations one address at a
+// time.
+//
+// The race starts one connect per resolved address at once, with no delay
+// between them and no cap. On a healthy host that costs a few redundant sockets
+// and buys some latency. On a hostname whose addresses do not answer it costs
+// one socket per address per attempt, all held for the full dial timeout, and
+// the winner cache never fills because a winner is only ever recorded on
+// success -- so every retry pays the full fan-out again. Measured on Android
+// against a peer-to-peer CDN hostname with two dead peers, that was 470
+// simultaneous half-open sockets for 235 connections.
+//
+// Defaulting this off restores the upstream one-at-a-time behaviour for DIRECT
+// without giving up the androidcyaml facade, which only exists on dev. Flip it
+// back to true to re-enable the race once it throttles its candidates.
+var DirectRaceEnabled = false
+
 // WithDirectDualStack enables the asynchronous A/AAAA and TCP connection race
 // used by the DIRECT outbound. It is intentionally separate from the generic
 // dual-stack dialer so DNS upstream hostnames keep their existing behavior.
